  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R13", "R14"],
      "pins": ["J1.3", "J10.3"],
      "nets": ["NET0030", "NET0017"],
      "observed": "On the right-hand servo-signal block the two jumper resistors that tie the two servo header signal pins (J1 pin3 / J10 pin3) together onto the shared bus node are drawn with a value of 4 kΩ (the vertical resistors labelled R13/R14, each annotated '4k'), sitting between NET0030 and NET0017.",
      "expected": "These are the serial-servo bridge jumpers, which the specification defines as 0 Ω and which the on-sheet note explicitly says must be shorted: 'Short R13, R14 for Serial Servo' / 'R13, R14 | 0 Ω (jumpers) | When shorted, bridge J3-pin3 and J8-pin3 together and onto the MAX3485 TTL bus'. No 4 kΩ value appears anywhere in the design (the resistor set is 0 Ω, 1 kΩ, 2.2 kΩ, 5.1 kΩ, 10 kΩ, 20 kΩ, 47 kΩ).",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Right-side servo block: vertical resistors R13 and R14 shown with value '4k', between NET0030 and NET0017; note 'Short R13, R14 for Serial Servo' (page 1, sheet /, m5-pantilt.kicad_sch)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "R13, R14 | 0 Ω (jumpers) | When shorted, bridge J3-pin3 and J8-pin3 together and onto the MAX3485 TTL bus — schematic note: 'Short R13, R14 for Serial Servo'."}
      ],
      "impact": "In serial-servo mode the two signal pins are not low-ohm bridged to the MAX3485 bus; a 4 kΩ series element in the shared TTL line heavily attenuates the half-duplex signal, so the RS-485 transceiver cannot reliably drive/read the serial servos. In PWM mode the same position is also wrong (4 kΩ instead of the intended 1 kΩ series buffer), degrading the servo signal edges.",
      "confidence": 0.6
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic's reference designators are wholesale renumbered relative to the spec, but the topology and most values match; the one electrical deviation is the pair of serial-servo bridge jumpers (R13/R14) being drawn as 4 kΩ instead of the required 0 Ω, which breaks the shared serial bus (and mis-sets the PWM series resistance)."
}

